// prelude.sets
//
// The domains that this folder uses beyond base. Read it with the value model.
//
// The domains in this folder describe data as a program holds it. They do not
// describe a serialized form, and none is implied.
//
// From base, as the value model describes them: U, union, intersect, String,
// Integer, Fractional, Boolean, Nothing, Float64, Seq, SetOf, Map, and the
// atom NotANumber. Float64 also brings the atoms PositiveInfinity,
// NegativeInfinity and NegativeZero. Everything else used here is declared here.
//
// Two domains here are described subdomains, Finite and NumericKey. Each has
// its exact members in the "Extent:" comment above it. They overlap other
// domains as their extents say.
//
// This folder was written without reading base itself. If base already
// declares one of the names below, that is a clash to resolve, not a second
// meaning.


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1. Numbers
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// A Fractional with no fractional part: 1.0, -2.0, 0.0.
// n is local to the rule, so the rule reads: some Integer n is equal in size
// to x. 1.0 is a member, 1 is not (it is an Integer), 1.5 is not.
WholeFractional = { x suchthat x in Fractional and n in Integer and x >= n and x <= n }

// The numbers of the 64-bit binary floating-point format, each written in
// exactly one way.
//
// Float64 holds a small whole number in two forms, 2 and 2.0. Double keeps one:
//   a whole number is in Integer form       0   1   -7   1000000000000000000000
//   any other finite number is Fractional   0.5   -2.25
//   the four special values are atoms       NotANumber   PositiveInfinity
//                                           NegativeInfinity   NegativeZero
// So 1 is a member and 1.0 is not, 0 is a member and 0.0 is not, and one
// stored number is always one value. Whoever writes a stored number as a
// member of Double writes a whole number without a decimal point.
//
// A member is an exact number, so its spelling can be long. The stored number
// nearest to one tenth is
//   0.1000000000000000055511151231257827021181583404541015625
// and that is a member. 0.1 is not: no stored number equals it.
//
// The Integer form is a spelling, not a kind. A domain declared as Double
// holds fractions too, and the source had no separate whole-number kind for
// it. Only a roster of whole numbers, such as {0, 1, 2}, is known to hold
// whole numbers only.
Double = { x suchthat x in Float64 and x not in WholeFractional }


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 2. A second kind of nothing
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// base has one nothing, Nothing: a value that is there and is deliberately
// empty. unset is another kind: what a slot holds when it was never given a
// value. unset is an ordinary atom. It is not a member of Phi, it is equal
// only to itself, and it is not Nothing.
//
// So a field can lack a usable value in three ways, and they are three
// different records:
//   {("name", "a")}                     no tuple: the field is missing
//   {("name", "a"), ("age", unset)}     the field is there and holds unset
//   {("name", "a"), ("age", Nothing)}   the field is there and holds Nothing
// A domain says which of the three it admits:
//   r in OptionalField(k, V)   admits the missing field
//   Unset inside V             admits unset
//   {Nothing} inside V         admits Nothing
//
// unset is a kind of absence, not a piece of data. A target may hold it the
// way it holds absence, as long as the states that one domain admits stay
// distinct.
Unset = {unset}


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 3. An instant
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// A point in time: a whole number of milliseconds counted from
// 1970-01-01T00:00:00 UTC, with every day taken as 86400000 milliseconds.
// Negative numbers are instants before that moment. The range is one hundred
// million days either side of it. NotANumber is the one invalid instant.
// Every member is also a Double.
EpochMillis = union({ x suchthat x in Integer and x >= -8640000000000000 and x <= 8640000000000000 }, {NotANumber})


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 4. Finite sets, maps and records
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Extent: a value is a member exactly when it is a domain and it has finitely
// many members, so that counting them comes to an end.
//   members:      {}   {1, 2}   {("a", 1)}   {{}}
//   not members:  Integer   String   Seq(Integer)     their members never run out
//                 1   "a"   (1, 2)   unset   Nothing  they are not domains
// () is a member, because () and {} are treated as one value.
// The SetOf and Map of base also hold endless domains. Every set, map and
// record in this folder is finite, and says so through Finite.
Finite sub U

// A finite set of members of T. {} is the empty set.
FiniteSet({T sub U}): intersect(SetOf(T), Finite)

// A finite map: (key, value) pairs with unique keys, every key in K and every
// value in V. {} is the empty map.
//
// Used on its own, as in X = FiniteMap(K, V), it is a table: the keys are
// data, and a member can have any number of them.
// Used as the first clause of a record builder, r in FiniteMap(String, V), it
// says that every field of the record, named or not, has a value in V.
FiniteMap({K sub U}, {V sub U}): intersect(Map(K, V), Finite)

// A finite record whose keys are all Strings. Its values can be anything.
//
// Every member is a Record. In this folder, r in Object stands where the value
// model writes r in Record as the first clause of a record builder. A builder
// that starts this way names its fields: those keys are structure, not data.
//
// No record in this folder is closed, and none uses OnlyKeys. A member may
// carry keys that its domain does not name, with any value. The source permits
// such keys and says nothing more about them. They are always Strings, because
// the record is an Object.
Object = FiniteMap(String, U)

// Extent: a String t is a member exactly when t is the key text of some
// member x of Double. Every member of Double has exactly one key text:
//   NotANumber has "NaN", PositiveInfinity has "Infinity", and
//   NegativeInfinity has "-Infinity".
//   0 and NegativeZero both have "0".
//   If x < 0, it is "-" followed by the key text of 0 - x.
//   If x > 0, take the shortest run of decimal digits d1 d2 ... dk, with d1
//   not 0, and the Integer n, for which x is the member of Double nearest to
//   the number 0.d1d2...dk * 10 ^ n. Of two equally near members, the nearest
//   is the one that is an even multiple of the difference between them. If
//   several runs of that length qualify, take the run whose number is nearest
//   to x, and of two equally near runs the one whose last digit is even.
//   Then the key text is:
//     if k <= n and n <= 21     the digits, then n - k zeros
//     if 0 < n and n < k        the first n digits, ".", the other digits
//     if -6 < n and n <= 0      "0.", then 0 - n zeros, then the digits
//     otherwise                 d1, then "." and the other digits if k > 1,
//                               then "e", then "+" if n > 21 or "-" if not,
//                               then n - 1 in decimal digits, without a sign
//   members:      "0"  "1"  "-7"  "1.5"  "0.001"  "1500"  "NaN"  "Infinity"
//                 "1e+21"  "1.5e-7"  "9007199254740992"
//   not members:  "-0"  "01"  "1.0"  "+1"  "1e21"  ".5"  " 1"  "x"  ""
// A check can accept "NaN", "Infinity" and "-Infinity" first. For any other
// t, it reads t as a decimal number, rounds it to the nearest member of
// Double, writes that member's key text, and compares the result with t.
//
// These Strings are the keys under which a number is filed in a record. Two
// different members of Double never share a key text, except 0 and
// NegativeZero.
NumericKey sub String


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 5. Broad domains
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Every structured value: a sequence, or a finite domain, which is a set, a
// map or a record. What is inside it can be anything.
// An EpochMillis is a number, so it is not a member.
Composite = union(Seq(U), Finite)

// Every value that is not a kind of nothing.
// Integer is listed beside Double because the source also has whole numbers
// of any size. The two share every whole number that Double can hold: such a
// number is one value, whichever of the two it was drawn from.
Something = union(String, union(Double, union(Integer, union(Boolean, Composite))))

// Every value there is in this folder: something, Nothing, or unset.
// Not members: any other atom, such as red; a tuple that is not a sequence,
// such as (1) or (1, 2); and a Callable placeholder, which is not data.
Anything = union(Something, union({Nothing}, Unset))


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 6. Things that only exist while a program runs
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// A runtime domain, in the sense of the value model: not data. A target never
// serializes a member and never checks one. It binds the domain to its own
// kind of routine.
//
// Callable(P, R) stands for a routine that is called with a list of arguments
// and gives back one value.
//   P is the domain of the argument list: a sequence with one position for
//     each argument. For a routine called with a String and then a Double it
//     is { (a, (b, ())) suchthat a in String and b in Double }.
//   R is the domain of what comes back. It is Unset for a routine that gives
//     nothing back.
//
// The domain is a tag. Its one member, (callable, P, R), is a placeholder that
// says nothing except which domain it is in. Where a record has a field in a
// Callable domain, the field holds some routine, and the placeholder stands
// for it. Two Callable domains are equal exactly when their P and their R are
// equal.
//
// The name and the tag are this folder's own. If the reader's standards
// already have a runtime domain for routines, it is the same idea.
Callable({P sub U}, {R sub U}): {(callable, P, R)}
